  return openBlock(), createElementBlock("div", null, [..._cache[0] || (_cache[0] = [
    createStaticVNode('<h1 id="_3-1-basic-concepts-of-artificial-intelligence" tabindex="-1">3.1 Basic Concepts of Artificial Intelligence <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-basic-concepts-of-artificial-intelligence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1 Basic Concepts of Artificial Intelligence&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="_3-1-1-lesson-introduction" tabindex="-1">3.1.1 Lesson Introduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-1-lesson-introduction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1.1 Lesson Introduction&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Artificial intelligence stands at the core of today&#39;s technology landscape. It has already become deeply integrated into daily life, with applications visible everywhere: face-scan payments, voice assistants, smart recommendations, and Go-playing AI. Yet most people hold significant misconceptions about AI, often conflating it with robots or intelligent life forms.</p><p>This lesson serves as the foundational entry point. It deconstructs the essential nature of artificial intelligence from the ground up, compares traditional programs with AI, traces the origins and development of AI, and maps out its capability systems and hierarchical classifications. These elements build a complete foundational framework for understanding AI and prepare learners for subsequent topics, including AI recognition, AI training, and future developments in artificial intelligence. The lesson avoids complex technical terminology and relies on accessible explanations and concrete examples. Complete beginners can quickly grasp what artificial intelligence truly means.</p><h2 id="_3-1-2-learning-objectives" tabindex="-1">3.1.2 Learning Objectives <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-2-learning-objectives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1.2 Learning Objectives&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p><strong>Cognitive Objectives</strong>: Clarify common misconceptions regarding artificial intelligence. Distinguish between AI, robotics, and intelligent life forms, and understand standard definitions and core capabilities.</p></li><li><p><strong>Knowledge Objectives</strong>: Understand the development history, three major technical schools, and three core pillars of AI. Distinguish the operational logic of traditional programs from artificial intelligence.</p></li><li><p><strong>Skill Objectives</strong>: Identify everyday AI applications and distinguish between the three AI tiers: weak AI, strong AI, and super AI.</p></li><li><p><strong>Literacy Objectives</strong>: Build an objective perspective toward AI without overstatement or bias, fostering scientific and technological literacy.</p></li></ol><h2 id="_3-1-3-learning-practice" tabindex="-1">3.1.3 Learning Practice <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-3-learning-practice" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1.3 Learning Practice&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_1-core-definition-of-artificial-intelligence" tabindex="-1"><strong>(1) Core Definition of Artificial Intelligence</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-core-definition-of-artificial-intelligence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**(1) Core Definition of Artificial Intelligence**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Artificial intelligence, abbreviated as AI, carries this core meaning: through computers, sensors, and algorithmic programs, machines simulate and replicate human behavior patterns and thought processes.</p><p>  In practical terms, this concept breaks down into two core capabilities:</p><p>  <strong>① Thinking like humans: Machines possess the ability to judge, analyze, and reason.</strong></p><p>  <strong>② Acting like humans: Machines perceive external environments and complete tasks autonomously.</strong></p><h3 id="_2-five-core-simulation-capabilities-of-artificial-intelligence" tabindex="-1"><strong>(2) Five Core Simulation Capabilities of Artificial Intelligence</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-five-core-simulation-capabilities-of-artificial-intelligence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**(2) Five Core Simulation Capabilities of Artificial Intelligence**&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The core logic of AI is replicating human intelligence. This corresponds to five core capabilities that cover the vast majority of AI application scenarios:</p><p>  1. <strong>Perception capability</strong>: Corresponds to human visual and auditory senses. Core technologies include computer vision and speech recognition. Typical applications: face-scan payment, photo-based object identification, and speech-to-text conversion.</p><img loading="lazy" height="1600" width="2848" src="' + _imports_0 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><p>  2. <strong>Language comprehension capability</strong>: Corresponds to human language communication abilities. Core technology is natural language processing. Typical applications: AI dialogue, machine translation, and intelligent copywriting.</p><img loading="lazy" height="1600" width="2848" src="' + _imports_1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><p>  3. <strong>Autonomous learning capability</strong>: Corresponds to human abilities to accumulate experience and acquire new knowledge. Core technologies include machine learning and deep learning. A typical application is AI autonomously distinguishing cats from dogs through massive image datasets and continuously optimizing recognition accuracy.</p><img loading="lazy" height="1600" width="2848" src="' + _imports_2 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><p>  4. <strong>Logical reasoning capability</strong>: Corresponds to human logical thinking and analytical judgment abilities. This is implemented through algorithms and knowledge bases. Typical applications: AI mathematical problem-solving, intelligent retrieval, and case analysis assistance.</p><img loading="lazy" height="1600" width="2848" src="' + _imports_3 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><p>  5. <strong>Autonomous decision-making capability</strong>: Corresponds to human judgment and choice abilities. Core technology is reinforcement learning. Typical applications: AlphaGo Go matches, autonomous driving obstacle avoidance, and intelligent route planning.</p><img loading="lazy" height="1600" width="2848" src="' + _imports_4 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><p>  <strong>In plain terms, AI systems possess the capabilities to listen, see, perceive, speak, act, and reason.</strong></p><h3 id="_3-artificial-intelligence-in-daily-life" tabindex="-1"><strong>(3) Artificial Intelligence in Daily Life</strong> <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-artificial-intelligence-in-daily-life" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;**(3) Artificial Intelligence in Daily Life**&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><strong>Campus Classroom Scenario</strong></li></ol><p>AI-powered interactive smartboard: Built-in image recognition and speech recognition AI enable voice-activated retrieval of courseware, automatic grading of multiple-choice questions, and conversion of handwritten board content to digital text. The system analyzes student response data to identify knowledge gaps and pushes corresponding exercises. These functions demonstrate core AI capabilities in data analysis and autonomous content adaptation.</p><img loading="lazy" height="317" width="448" src="' + _imports_5 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><ol start="2"><li><strong>Home Living Scenario</strong></li></ol><p>  ① Intelligent Robot Vacuum: Equipped with LiDAR and vision systems, the device autonomously maps an entire living space and detects obstacles such as furniture, shoes, and power cords. It dynamically adjusts suction levels based on floor conditions and logs optimal cleaning paths. With repeated use, the robot continuously refines its spatial mapping through autonomous learning.</p><img loading="lazy" height="307" width="448" src="' + _imports_6 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><p>  ② Large-screen smart refrigerator: Built-in camera AI identifies ingredients inside the refrigerator, automatically records expiration dates, and pushes reminders. Based on stored ingredients, the system intelligently recommends recipes. Voice interaction through a connected smartphone enables remote temperature adjustment. These functions rely on image recognition and big data analysis to deliver intelligent services.</p><img loading="lazy" height="336" width="448" src="' + _imports_7 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><ol start="3"><li><strong>Urban Road Scenario</strong></li></ol><p>Autonomous driving taxi: Multiple cameras and LiDAR AI recognize traffic lights, pedestrians, non-motorized vehicles, and road signs in real time. The system autonomously plans driving routes. When detecting pedestrians crossing the road, the system automatically slows down and yields. Throughout the journey, the system independently judges road conditions without human steering wheel control.</p><img loading="lazy" height="253" width="448" src="' + _imports_8 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><ol start="4"><li><strong>Hospital Medical Scenario</strong></li></ol><p>Hospital service robot: Speech dialogue AI understands patient questions and autonomously guides them to clinics and payment windows. Based on symptoms, the system intelligently recommends corresponding departments. The robot records high-frequency questions and continuously optimizes response logic. This achieves autonomous interaction and autonomous navigation.</p><img loading="lazy" height="309" width="448" src="' + _imports_9 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><ol start="5"><li><strong>Shopping Mall and Restaurant Scenario</strong></li></ol><p>Food delivery robot: Indoor visual navigation enables automatic identification of table numbers and avoidance of moving customers. The robot completes food delivery and plate collection tasks, autonomously returning to the pickup area. When encountering obstacles, the robot automatically stops and reroutes. This adapts to complex restaurant environments.</p><img loading="lazy" height="224" width="448" src="' + _imports_10 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><h2 id="_3-1-4-extended-knowledge" tabindex="-1">3.1.4 Extended Knowledge <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-4-extended-knowledge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1.4 Extended Knowledge&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>(1) Origins of Artificial Intelligence</strong></p><ul><li><p><strong>Theoretical foundation</strong>: In 1950, Alan Turing published <em>Computing Machinery and Intelligence</em>, proposing the classic &quot;Turing Test&quot; and establishing the core theoretical foundation for the feasibility of artificial intelligence.</p></li><li><p><strong>Official birth</strong>: At the 1956 Dartmouth Conference, leading scientists including John McCarthy and Marvin Minsky first proposed the term &quot;artificial intelligence,&quot; marking the formal establishment of AI as an independent scientific discipline.</p></li></ul><p><strong>(2) Impact of Artificial Intelligence on Human Life</strong></p><ul><li>More convenient living: Intelligent voice assistants, food delivery algorithms, navigation, face-scan payment, and smart homes simplify daily chores and save time.</li><li>Enhanced work and production efficiency: Office AI assists with copywriting and data organization. Intelligent factory robots and agricultural smart equipment reduce repetitive labor and drive industrial upgrading.</li><li>Improved healthcare and education: AI-assisted imaging diagnosis and the development of new drugs. Personalized learning tools enable teaching according to individual aptitude, lowering the threshold for accessing quality resources.</li><li>Enriched entertainment: AI painting, video generation, and intelligent music and film recommendations create entirely new content formats.</li></ul><p><strong>(3) Future Trends in Artificial Intelligence</strong></p><ul><li><p>Multimodal intelligent fusion: Current AI mostly processes only single modalities such as images or voice. Future AI will simultaneously comprehend visuals, understand speech, read text, and recognize actions. This enables comprehensive perception of the world and more natural interaction.</p></li><li><p>Deep integration across all industries: AI will no longer remain confined to home appliances and robots. It will penetrate deeply into industrial manufacturing, agricultural cultivation, aerospace, and urban management. Artificial intelligence will enhance productivity across all of society.</p></li><li><p>Human-robot collaboration becomes the mainstream model: AI will not completely replace human work. Instead, it will serve as an assistive tool. Humans will handle creativity, emotion, and complex decision-making. AI will handle repetitive calculation, environmental perception, and physical labor. The two will divide tasks and cooperate.</p></li><li><p>Exploration toward general artificial intelligence: Current AI consists of &quot;narrow AI.&quot; One machine can only perform one task. A robot vacuum only vacuums. In the future, scientists will research general AI. A single intelligent system will flexibly complete diverse tasks including learning, service, creation, and inspection.</p></li></ul><h2 id="_3-1-5-q-a" tabindex="-1">3.1.5 Q&amp;A <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-5-q-a" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1.5 Q&amp;A&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Question 1: What are some common weak AI applications in daily life?</strong></p><p>Answer:① Face-scan payment AI has a dedicated task: recognizing faces and completing identity verification. It can only perform face recognition and comparison. ② AI translation tools have a dedicated task: multilingual text and voice translation. They focus exclusively on language conversion. ③ short-video recommendation AI: Its dedicated task is pushing corresponding videos based on viewer preferences. It is only responsible for content recommendation. All three can only complete single-domain tasks and cannot work across domains. These are typical examples of weak artificial intelligence.</p><p><strong>Question 2: Why does current artificial intelligence lack true autonomous thinking capabilities?</strong></p><p>Answer: All current AI systems are tools driven by data, algorithms, and computing power. AI possesses no self-awareness, subjective thinking, or emotions. AI merely summarizes patterns from massive datasets and imitates human behavior. It completes tasks according to preset models. AI cannot autonomously comprehend the meaning of tasks, cannot think independently, and cannot exercise subjective judgment. It does not truly possess human-like consciousness or thinking capabilities.</p><p><strong>Question 3: Is an intelligent robot vacuum an AI carrier or AI itself?</strong></p><p>Answer: It is an AI hardware carrier. The robot vacuum is a physical hardware device. The algorithmic programs for path planning, obstacle avoidance, and cleaning recognition that run inside it constitute artificial intelligence itself. The robot is merely the shell that carries the AI algorithms and implements intelligent cleaning functions. Without the AI algorithms, it is only ordinary mechanical equipment.</p><h2 id="_3-1-6-technical-support-and-product-discussion" tabindex="-1">3.1.6 Technical Support and Product Discussion <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-1-6-technical-support-and-product-discussion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.1.6 Technical Support and Product Discussion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>If creative ideas emerge after using the product, sharing them in the <a href="https://forum.hiwonder.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hiwonder community</a> is welcome:</p><a href="https://forum.hiwonder.com.cn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img loading="lazy" height="640" width="1136" src="' + _imports_12 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"></a>', 60)
  ])]);
